<p>If you own a home or building in San Marcos, hail is not a question of if, but when. San Marcos hail season arrives every spring as storms roll off the Hill Country and across the I-35 corridor, and some years leave many roofs across Hays County needing inspection. This article walks through what NOAA's storm records actually show for Hays County: how many hail reports there have been, which months are busiest, and which storms produced the largest stones. Then we cover what that history means for your roof, your deductible, and your insurance claim.</p>

<p>Everything below comes from the NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a>. Where we mention a specific storm, we link to the official event record so you can read it yourself. You can also browse local events on our <a href="/storm-history/">San Marcos storm history</a> page.</p>

<h2>Hays County hail by the numbers</h2>

<p>The NCEI database contains 180 hail reports for Hays County from 1967 through 2026 (2026 data is still preliminary). A "report" is one observation of hail at one location. A single large storm can generate several reports as it moves across the county, so these counts measure how often hail is observed, not how many separate storm days there were.</p>

<table>
