<tr><td>Any size</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>180</td></tr>
<tr><td>1 inch or larger</td><td>Quarter</td><td>133</td></tr>
<tr><td>1.75 inches or larger</td><td>Golf ball</td><td>58</td></tr>
<tr><td>2.75 inches or larger</td><td>Baseball</td><td>8</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The 1-inch line matters because that is roughly where the National Weather Service considers hail severe. About three out of four Hays County reports meet or exceed it. Nearly one in three reaches golf-ball size, which is large enough to bruise or fracture many composition shingles and dent metal roofing, gutters, and HVAC fins.</p>

<p>The record has also gotten busier. Reporting has improved over the decades as more spotters, cameras, and phones came into use, so older years are probably undercounted. Even so, recent seasons stand out: Hays County logged 15 hail reports in 2024, 9 in 2023, and 9 in 2025. Rapid growth along I-35 in San Marcos, Kyle, and Buda means more roofs sit under each storm path than they did a generation ago.</p>

<h2>San Marcos hail season by month</h2>

<p>Here is every Hays County hail report in the database grouped by month. The pattern is clear: hail is a spring problem in San Marcos.</p>

