<p>March through May produced 147 of the 180 reports, about 82%. April and May alone account for nearly 70%. That lines up with what we see in claims: spring is when warm, humid Gulf air meets dry air and passing upper-level systems over the Balcones Escarpment, and the storms that form along that boundary can grow tall enough to produce large hail quickly.</p>

<p>Two practical takeaways. First, late winter is a good time to have your roof looked at and photographed, so you have a "before" record going into the season. Second, if you notice damage in May, do not assume it came from the most recent storm. Several hail days can stack up within a few weeks, and the date of loss you report should match the storm that actually caused the damage.</p>

