<h2>What hail history means for San Marcos roofs</h2>

<p>The storm record helps explain why hail claims are so common here, and why they can get complicated.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Composition shingles take the brunt.</strong> Most subdivisions in San Marcos, Kyle, and Buda have asphalt shingle roofs. Hail around 1 inch and up can knock off granules, crack the mat, and break the seal strips, even when the damage is hard to see from the ground.</li>
<li><strong>Metal roofs dent, and the policy matters.</strong> Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs are common on Hill Country homes, barns, and outbuildings toward Wimberley and Dripping Springs. Some policies carry "cosmetic damage" exclusions that limit coverage for dents that do not affect function. Read that endorsement before you file.</li>
<li><strong>Soft metals and other components.</strong> Gutters, downspouts, vents, AC condenser fins, window screens, and painted wood or siding often show hail marks. They also help confirm hail size and direction.</li>
<li><strong>Repeated storms raise the question of old damage.</strong> When a roof has been through several hail seasons, insurers may argue that some damage is old or wear-related. A pre-season inspection and dated photos are the best answer to that argument.</li>
</ul>

<p>For a deeper look at roof-specific claims, see our guide to <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Wind/hail deductibles in Hays County</h2>

<p>Hays County is not in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) area, so San Marcos homes and businesses usually get wind and hail coverage from their regular insurer, or from the Texas FAIR Plan in some cases. Many of those policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible that is a percentage of the dwelling (Coverage A) limit rather than a flat dollar figure.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage A limit</th><th>1% deductible</th><th>2% deductible</th><th>3% deductible</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$250,000</td><td>$2,500</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$7,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$350,000</td><td>$3,500</td><td>$7,000</td><td>$10,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$450,000</td><td>$4,500</td><td>$9,000</td><td>$13,500</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Those figures are simple math, not quotes. Check your declarations page to find your own deductible. Also look for roof payment schedules or actual cash value (ACV) roof endorsements, which some Texas policies use and which can reduce what is paid on an older roof. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> can help you estimate where you stand after the deductible.</p>

<h2>How to document hail damage in San Marcos</h2>

<p>Good documentation is the difference between a claim that moves and one that stalls. Here is a practical checklist; our full <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> goes further.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Write down the date and time.</strong> Note when the hail fell and how long it lasted. Then confirm it against official records using our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> or the <a href="/weather-events/">recent weather events</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph hailstones with a size reference.</strong> A coin, ruler, or tape measure next to fresh stones is useful evidence. Do it quickly; hail melts fast in a Texas spring.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph from the ground first.</strong> Gutters, downspouts, window screens, fence caps, siding, vehicles, and AC units. Stay off the roof unless it is safe; a fall is never worth it.</li>
<li><strong>Check the interior.</strong> Look for new ceiling stains, attic leaks, or wet insulation, and photograph them with the date.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage.</strong> Most policies require reasonable steps to protect the property, such as tarping an active leak. Keep receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> with the specific storm date, and keep a log of every call, email, and inspection.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Know the timelines</span><p>Under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act, insurers generally must acknowledge a claim and begin investigating within 15 days of notice, then accept or reject within 15 business days after receiving the information they requested, with possible extensions. After a declared weather catastrophe, these deadlines can be extended. See the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/storms/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance storm resources</a> for more.</p></div>

<h2>Where a San Marcos public adjuster fits in</h2>

<p>Storm data and photos are a starting point. The harder part is turning them into a complete, line-by-line estimate and getting the insurer to engage with it. A <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> works for the policyholder, not the insurance company. We can review your policy, inspect and document the roof and other components, prepare a detailed estimate, and handle communication with the carrier. When an insurer's scope leaves items out, we can submit <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements/">hail damage claim supplements</a>, and when the disagreement is only about the amount of loss, the policy's <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal clause</a> may be an option.</p>

<p>We cannot promise a particular outcome; every claim depends on the damage and the policy. What we can do is help make sure the claim is presented accurately and completely. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins, and Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<p>If spring storms have left you wondering about your roof, start with our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">San Marcos hail damage claims</a> page or request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a>. As a San Marcos Public Adjuster serving San Marcos, Hays County, and surrounding Central Texas communities, we are happy to look at your situation and tell you plainly whether we think we can help.</p>

